- op: discuss  # bfc7
  at: '2026-08-02T19:08:24Z'  # bfc7
  by: agent  # bfc7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bfc7
  clock: 5  # bfc7
  nonce: bfc7  # bfc7
  message: "FIXED and merged, PR #167, dev c539caf0b7. Ships with WI-ninar - neither is safe\nalone.\n\nTHE AUDIT FOUND FOUR SITES, not the one filed:\n  merge-pr:297     enumerated 1,2   -> merged on exit 3 AS SHIPPED\n  auto-pr:2065     enumerated 1,2   -> merged on exit 3 AS SHIPPED\n                                       (the POST-REBASE merge path)\n  auto-pr:1349     enumerated 3,1,2 -> would fall through on any new code\n  auto-pr:1996     enumerated 3,1,2 -> would fall through on any new code\n  forgejo-api:1474 proceeds only rc==0 -> already SAFE, unchanged\n  forgejo-api:1819 returns 0 only rc==0 -> already SAFE, unchanged\nThe two safe sites were safe for exactly the reason L54 gives: they enumerate\nthe PERMITTING case. That is the argument for making it the only spelling.\n\nFIX: ci_verdict_permits_merge in the forge library is the single home for \"may\nwe merge on this verdict?\" - 0 permits, every other value refuses and preserves\nits code, an unrecognised verdict says so rather than refusing silently. Not\n\"also handle 3\", which reproduces the same shape one code later. Per-site\nrecovery text stays at the call sites (L27); retry-loop conditions are untouched\nbecause \"should we retry?\" is a different question.\n\nRoot-cause note worth keeping: poll_ci's own header comment documented only\n\"0 = success, 1 = failure, 2 = timeout\" and never mentioned 3, which is\nplausibly how four call sites came to omit it. Comment fixed, and it now records\nthat callers may not depend on it being complete.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE: the helper is exercised directly from the shipped library\nfor 0, 1, 2, 3, three unallocated codes and a non-numeric value; CI_VERDICT_STATE\nis asserted for each. Mutation-tested two ways - making unknown verdicts permit,\nand re-hand-rolling merge-pr's gate - each turns exactly the corresponding test\nred. PR #167 then merged THROUGH the new gate on verdict 0, so the permitting\npath is exercised end to end in production.\n\nNOT OBSERVED: a production REFUSAL. No non-zero verdict has occurred since the\nfix, so the refusal path is test-proven rather than field-proven.\n\nBRANCH PROTECTION, the second half of this item, now answered rather than\nassumed. The required check ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker was configured correctly\non both dev and main; \"Do not allow bypassing the above settings\" was UNTICKED,\nso the rules did not apply to the merging account - the only way a merge\nsucceeds against a pending required check. The human has ticked it on both, so\nthere are now two independent barriers: the script-side chokepoint and a\nserver-side rule that no longer exempts us."  # bfc7
